WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:05.140
Secure ASP.NET Core development is not a checklist at the end of a sprint.

2
00:00:05.240 --> 00:00:14.520
It is a way of engineering the application so security decisions are visible in the design, clear in the code, supported by hosting configuration, and observable in production.

3
00:00:14.620 --> 00:00:25.020
The framework provides useful security features, but the team still decides where trust changes, what must be validated, who can perform an operation, and what should happen when assumptions fail.

4
00:00:25.300 --> 00:00:29.540
A useful mental model is the request pipeline.

5
00:00:29.640 --> 00:00:43.640
Every request moves through hosting infrastructure, proxy behavior, middleware, routing, authentication, authorization, endpoint code, model binding, data access, logging, and response generation.

6
00:00:43.740 --> 00:00:47.260
Security-sensitive behavior can occur at any of those points.

7
00:00:47.360 --> 00:00:58.800
A small configuration change in the pipeline can affect error exposure, authentication handling, cross-origin behavior, routing, static files, headers, or how an endpoint is reached.

8
00:00:59.080 --> 00:01:02.280
Trust boundaries help organize that thinking.

9
00:01:02.380 --> 00:01:15.740
A browser, mobile client, partner API, identity provider, background worker, database, file store, queue, administrator console, and operations tool may all make different assumptions.

10
00:01:15.840 --> 00:01:28.000
When data, identity, commands, files, or configuration cross those boundaries, the application should treat them as untrusted or at least unverified until they are checked for the current purpose.

11
00:01:28.280 --> 00:01:47.320
ASP.NET Core gives teams strong building blocks: authentication middleware, policy-based authorization, antiforgery protections, cookie options, Data Protection, model validation, encoding helpers, logging, configuration providers, dependency injection, and hosting abstractions.

12
00:01:47.420 --> 00:01:52.940
Those defaults can reduce common mistakes, but defaults are not a design review.

13
00:01:53.040 --> 00:02:00.480
Developers still need to choose appropriate settings, remove development-only behavior, and test how the pieces work together.

14
00:02:00.760 --> 00:02:05.800
The goal is prevention, detection, maintainability, and review working together.

15
00:02:05.900 --> 00:02:12.940
Good security controls should make the application harder to misuse, easier to monitor, and easier for future developers to reason about.

16
00:02:13.040 --> 00:02:24.720
Secure ASP.NET Core engineering is ultimately practical: build features that remain safe when users, inputs, environments, dependencies, and operational conditions change.
